### Phase 8: Shared Setup Architecture

The same code is used in both QEMU and physical environments, with `rc.local` automating what was previously manual:

**`router_config/setup.sh` (Shared Setup Functions)**
- Contains all configuration logic as individual shell functions
- No hardcoded execution order; designed to be sourced by other scripts
- Functions include: `create_dnscrypt_proxy_user()`, `install_packages()`, `install_dnscrypt_proxy()`, `copy_configs()`, `update_blocklists()`, `download_resolver_list()`, `wait_for_wan_interface()`, `start_services()`
- Can still be run manually if needed: `source router_config/setup.sh && create_dnscrypt_proxy_user`
- Works in both QEMU (diskless NFS boot) and physical (standard FreeBSD install) environments

**`pxe_boot_config/rc.local` (Unified Boot Orchestrator)**
- Sources `router_config/setup.sh` to import all setup functions
- Calls setup functions in order: user creation → package install → dnscrypt-proxy setup → config copy → blocklist update → resolver list → wait for WAN → start services
- Logs all output to `/var/log/rc.local.log` for debugging
- **Used in both environments:**
  - QEMU: Runs automatically on diskless NFS boot (part of playbook staging)
  - Physical: Added to `/etc/rc.local` after manual FreeBSD installation, ensures same setup path as emulated testing
- Single code path validates that testing environment matches production deployment

**`scripts/run_test_loop.sh` (QEMU Orchestration)**
- Orchestrates the full QEMU emulation flow
- Stages router configs via Ansible playbook (which copies rc.local and router_config/setup.sh to NFS root)
- Monitors boot completion via rc.local.log
- Isolated to test infrastructure only; not needed for physical deployment

